Xinliang An Department of Mathematics University of Toronto Toronto, M5S 2E4, Canada Department of Mathematics Rutgers University Piscataway, NJ 08854-8019, USA e-mail: xinliang.an@utoronto.ca Xuefeng Zhang TianQin Reseach Center for Gravitational Physics School of Physics and Astronomy Sun Yat-sen University Zhuhai, 519082, China Departments of Physics and Astronomy Beijing Normal University Beijing 100875, China e-mail: zhangxf38@sysu.edu.cn"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Zhang, X."},{"full_name":"Lü, H."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1325093"}}],"number_of_pages":33,"legacy_creation_date":"2015-09-29","preprint_date":"2015-09-26","author_count":2,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"34 pages, 5 figures; to match the published version which combines this number and arXiv:1509.07954"}],"earliest_date":"2015-09-26","refereed":true,"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"MSNET","value":"3748303"},{"schema":"ADS","value":"2018AnHP...19..619A"}],"facet_author_name":["1939597_Xingtao An","1942677_Xiao-Feng Zhang"],"core":true,"license":[{"imposing":"arXiv","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1395073","updated":"2025-08-13T07:53:45.398714"},"journal_title_variants":["Annales Henri Poincare"],"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc","hep-th"],"value":"1509.07956"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["N.Bedjaoui.1","R.B.Mann.1","J.H.Horne.1","M.W.Choptuik.1","C.M.Chambers.1","X.An.1","M.Reiterer.1","B.E.W.Nilsson.1","Kengo.Maeda.1","J.M.Overduin.1","J.M.M.Senovilla.1","J.Metzger.4","D.Garfinkle.1","S.Klainerman.1","R.Maartens.1","X.F.Zhang.2","A.M.Abrahams.1","R.Laflamme.1","S.J.Poletti.1","G.J.Galloway.1","P.Bizon.1","Michael.J.Duff.1","Hong.Lu.2","A.D.Rendall.1","A.Wasserman.1","E.P.Honda.1","P.Langfelder.2","P.Kraus.1","Kellogg.S.Stelle.1","Kazuya.Koyama.1","K.C.K.Chan.1","S.Mignemi.1","F.Pretorius.1","T.Piran.1","C.N.Pope.1","E.Gunzig.7","E.W.Hirschmann.1","L.Lehner.1","Lars.Andersson.1","B.J.Carr.1","R.Emparan.1","V.Faraoni.1","R.Schoen.2","Paul.K.Townsend.1","S.H.Hawley.2","An.Zhong.Wang.1","P.R.Brady.1","W.Simon.1","A.Rostworowski.1","T.Chmaj.1","I.Rodnianski.1","A.Feinstein.1","R.Penrose.1","C.R.Evans.1","B.G.Schmidt.1","Jerome.Novak.1","Yu.mei.Wu.1","C.Gundlach.1","D.L.Wiltshire.1","M.Bremer.1","V.V.Frolov.1","H.S.Reall.1","R.Gregory.1","Rong.Gen.Cai.1","D.S.Goldwirth.1","G.T.Horowitz.1","J.M.Martin.Garcia.1","P.Nardone.1","P.S.Wesson.1","M.Dafermos.1","P.G.LeFloch.1","T.T.Paetz.1","P.Figueras.1","J.J.Halliwell.1","M.Gutperle.1","Demetrios.Christodoulou.1","M.Kunesch.1","S.Tunyasuvunakool.1","J.Luk.1","E.Trubowitz.1","R.M.Wald.1","A.A.Coley.1","T.Hertog.1","S.M.C.V.Goncalves.3"],"figures":[{"filename":"1V.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=1.2$. The right plot shows the back view of the left. Green ($c_T&gt;\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=1$, cf. (\\ref{V0_cT})), blue, and cyan ($c_T&lt;\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=-1$) curves emanating from $\\mathcal{O}$ are members of the 1-parameter family of solutions of the system (\\ref{ode1}-\\ref{ode3}) subject to (\\ref{IV}) and (\\ref{IV1}). The negatively unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{O}$ and the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ are marked by red curves. The blue curve ($c_T=\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=0$) ending at $\\mathcal{P}_1$ delineates the unique massless solution. The maroon curve represents the set of singular points $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ with $\\zeta=1/2$.","source":"arxiv","key":"835f2398750fa1fb5a9805b80efa2cca","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/835f2398750fa1fb5a9805b80efa2cca"},{"filename":"1Va.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=1.2$. The right plot shows the back view of the left. Green ($c_T&gt;\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=1$, cf. (\\ref{V0_cT})), blue, and cyan ($c_T&lt;\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=-1$) curves emanating from $\\mathcal{O}$ are members of the 1-parameter family of solutions of the system (\\ref{ode1}-\\ref{ode3}) subject to (\\ref{IV}) and (\\ref{IV1}). The negatively unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{O}$ and the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ are marked by red curves. The blue curve ($c_T=\\kappa^2/3$, $V_0=0$) ending at $\\mathcal{P}_1$ delineates the unique massless solution. The maroon curve represents the set of singular points $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ with $\\zeta=1/2$.","source":"arxiv","key":"5f9653e0505cc4ded35fe4f84e54b261","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5f9653e0505cc4ded35fe4f84e54b261"},{"filename":"2V.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.5$ (roughly the front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Integral curves from $\\mathcal{O}$ may reach the singular curve $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ (maroon). Particularly, the blue ($V_0=0$) curve attaining $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ is the unique massless interior solution interpolating between green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves. In the region $\\zeta&gt;1/2$, curves drawn from $\\mathcal{P}_{\\pm}$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ are continuations of the interior solutions. The two purple curves terminating at $\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$ separate naked singularity solutions and black hole solutions. The red curve passing through $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) with one end reaching $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$ represents the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) in the massless phase subspace (\\ref{eqaml}).","source":"arxiv","key":"66ecdd1495ca04e06a3c205c03a4d83a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/66ecdd1495ca04e06a3c205c03a4d83a"},{"filename":"2Va.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.5$ (roughly the front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Integral curves from $\\mathcal{O}$ may reach the singular curve $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ (maroon). Particularly, the blue ($V_0=0$) curve attaining $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ is the unique massless interior solution interpolating between green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves. In the region $\\zeta&gt;1/2$, curves drawn from $\\mathcal{P}_{\\pm}$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ are continuations of the interior solutions. The two purple curves terminating at $\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$ separate naked singularity solutions and black hole solutions. The red curve passing through $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) with one end reaching $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$ represents the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) in the massless phase subspace (\\ref{eqaml}).","source":"arxiv","key":"e0412dc1b7284711e28dc67d7bbd389e","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e0412dc1b7284711e28dc67d7bbd389e"},{"filename":"2Vb.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.5$ (roughly the front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Integral curves from $\\mathcal{O}$ may reach the singular curve $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ (maroon). Particularly, the blue ($V_0=0$) curve attaining $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ is the unique massless interior solution interpolating between green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves. In the region $\\zeta&gt;1/2$, curves drawn from $\\mathcal{P}_{\\pm}$ and $\\mathcal{P}_{sc}$ are continuations of the interior solutions. The two purple curves terminating at $\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$ separate naked singularity solutions and black hole solutions. The red curve passing through $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) with one end reaching $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$ represents the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ ($\\mathcal{P}_{-1}$) in the massless phase subspace (\\ref{eqaml}).","source":"arxiv","key":"fe2adf7bd7726ff5eeead79f5c024665","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fe2adf7bd7726ff5eeead79f5c024665"},{"filename":"3V.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.7$ (roughly the upper front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Behavior of the green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves are similar to those in Fig. \\ref{fig2}. However, for massless solutions, the blue curves and the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ (red curve) no longer extend to the singular line $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$, indicating that naked singularities cease to occur.","source":"arxiv","key":"ad2efa53c09b2b4741ea5b632866cdc4","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ad2efa53c09b2b4741ea5b632866cdc4"},{"filename":"3Va.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.7$ (roughly the upper front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Behavior of the green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves are similar to those in Fig. \\ref{fig2}. However, for massless solutions, the blue curves and the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ (red curve) no longer extend to the singular line $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$, indicating that naked singularities cease to occur.","source":"arxiv","key":"5906d348b1693d211836cddcda736bfc","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5906d348b1693d211836cddcda736bfc"},{"filename":"3Vb.png","caption":"Numerical integration with $\\kappa=0.7$ (roughly the upper front and back views, and the projection onto the $y$-$\\zeta$ plane). Behavior of the green ($V_0=1$) and cyan ($V_0=-1$) curves are similar to those in Fig. \\ref{fig2}. However, for massless solutions, the blue curves and the unstable manifold of $\\mathcal{P}_1$ (red curve) no longer extend to the singular line $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$, indicating that naked singularities cease to occur.","source":"arxiv","key":"745d28f39a536960815cf8c4c0ef6f53","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/745d28f39a536960815cf8c4c0ef6f53"},{"filename":"4xru.png","caption":"A spacetime diagram of the naked singularity formation near the symmetry center. The self-similar spacetime is obtained by solving the autonomous system (\\ref{ode1}-\\ref{ode3}) from $x=0$ to $x=+\\infty$ (the coordinate line $x=-r/u$ sweeping from $r=0$ to $u=0$). The line $x=x_*$ ($x=+\\infty$) marks the past (future) light cones of the central curvature singularity at $r=0$, $u=0$. There is no apparent horizon. The curvature invariants (the Ricci and Kretschmann scalars) are well-behaved for $u&lt;0$ at the center and beyond.","source":"arxiv","key":"e96b40ec89037fe7c034207253fc5de3","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e96b40ec89037fe7c034207253fc5de3"},{"filename":"5V.png","caption":"Numerical integration of the autonomous system (\\ref{ode1}-\\ref{ode3}) with $V_0=-1$ and $\\kappa=1/\\sqrt{2}$. Integral curves from the initial point $\\mathcal{O}$ ($x=0$) first reach the singular curve $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$ ($x=x_*$, $\\zeta=1/2$), and then they bifurcate and continue to move towards the line $\\mathcal{P}_0(y)$ ($x=+\\infty$). The plot shows bifurcation drawn from one sampling point on $\\mathcal{P}_s(y)$.","source":"arxiv","key":"a177b2b598dbfdbf4df0b98e2807556d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/a177b2b598dbfdbf4df0b98e2807556d"}],"legacy_version":"20181120164947.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"},{"term":"Theory-HEP"}],"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.12981.33"},{"schema":"GRID","value":"grid.20513.35"},{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/022k4wk35"}],"full_name":"Zhang, Xuefeng","last_name":"Zhang","first_name":"Xuefeng","recid":1942677},"control_number":1395073,"dois":[{"source":"bibmatch","value":"10.1007/s00023-017-0631-9"},{"value":"10.1007/s00023-017-0631-9"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Zhang:2015rsa","An:2015ssa"],"deleted_records":[{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395074"}],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"The vacuum Einstein equations in $5+1$ dimensions are shown to admit solutions describing naked singularity formation in gravitational collapse from nonsingular asymptotically locally flat initial data that contain no trapped surface. We present a class of specific examples with topology $\\mathbb {R}^{3+1} \\times S^2$ . Thanks to the Kaluza–Klein dimensional reduction, these examples are constructed by lifting continuously self-similar solutions of the 4-dimensional Einstein-scalar field system with a negative exponential potential. The latter solutions are obtained by solving a 3-dimensional autonomous system of first-order ordinary differential equations with a combined analytic and numerical approach. Their existence provides a new test-bed for weak cosmic censorship in higher-dimensional gravity. In addition, we point out that a similar attempt of lifting Christodoulou’s naked singularity solutions of massless scalar fields fails to capture formation of naked singularities in $4+1$ dimensions, due to a diverging Kretschmann scalar in the initial data.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"The vacuum Einstein equations in 5+1 dimensions are shown to admit solutions describing naked singularity formation in gravitational collapse from nonsingular asymptotically locally flat initial data that contain no trapped surface. We present a class of specific examples with topology $\\mathbb{R}^{3+1} \\times S^2$. Thanks to the Kaluza-Klein dimensional reduction, these examples are constructed by lifting continuously self-similar solutions of the 4-dimensional Einstein-scalar field system with a negative exponential potential. The latter solutions are obtained by solving a 3-dimensional autonomous system of first-order ordinary differential equations with a combined analytic and numerical approach. Their existence provides a new test-bed for weak cosmic censorship in higher-dimensional gravity. In addition, we point out that a similar attempt of lifting Christodoulou's naked singularity solutions of massless scalar fields fails to capture formation of naked singularities in 4+1 dimensions, due to a diverging Kretschmann scalar in the initial data.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"Examples of naked singularity formation in higher-dimensional Einstein-vacuum spacetimes"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Naked singularity and black hole formation in self-similar Einstein-scalar fields with exponential potentials"}],"curated":true},"created":"2015-09-29T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1395073","updated":"2025-08-13T07:53:45.398714+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1395073?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1395073"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":20,"citation_count_without_self_citations":19,"authors":[{"raw_affiliations":[{"value":"Herzen Russian State Pedagogical U. - Moika 48 - St. Petersburg - 191186 - Russia"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"vertogradov, v.d.","full_name":"Vertogradov, V.D.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/2373188"},"last_name":"Vertogradov","ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"V.D.Vertogradov.1"}],"signature_block":"VARTAGRADAVv","uuid":"1adf19cc-5dcd-4e26-89a0-88572c51344b","first_name":"V.D.","recid":2373188}],"publication_info":[{"journal_volume":"22","page_end":"223","year":2016,"journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1213518"},"page_start":"220","journal_issue":"2","journal_title":"Grav.Cosmol."}],"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","references":[{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"29","page_end":"14510","year":2014,"page_start":"14501","journal_title":"Mod.Phys.Lett.A"},"title":{"title":"Geodesics with negative energy in the ergosphere of rotating black holes"},"misc":["Yu. V. Pavlov, and V. D. Vertogradov"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Grib, A.A."}]}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"21","page_end":"174","year":2015,"page_start":"171","journal_title":"Grav.Cosmol."},"title":{"title":"Geodesicswith negative energy in the ergosphere of rotating black holes"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Vertogradov, V.D."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1373613"}},{"reference":{"imprint":{"publisher":"Wiley"},"publication_info":{"year":1985},"misc":["in A Random Walk in Relativity and Cosmology Eastern, New Delhi,)"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Papapetrou, A."}]}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"year":2007},"misc":["Gravitational Collapse and Space-Time Singularities (Cambridge University Press,), p. 273"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Joshi, P.S."}]}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"1407.4309","title":{"title":"Gravitational collapse of generalized Vaidya spacetime"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Mkenyeley, M.D."},{"full_name":"Goswami, R."},{"full_name":"Maharaj, S.D."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1306607"}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"gr-qc/9903083","title":{"title":"Naked singularity of the Vaidya-de Sitter space-time and the cosmic censorship hypothesis"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Wagh, S.M."},{"full_name":"Maharaj, S.D."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/497096"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"60","artid":"024014","year":1999,"journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Nolan, B.C."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/495082"}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"gr-qc/9803038","title":{"title":"GeneralizedVaidya solutions"},"misc":["and Yu. Wu"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Wang, A."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/468035"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"64","artid":"8","year":1977,"page_start":"8","journal_title":"Phys.Lett.A"},"authors":[{"full_name":"Tipler, F.J."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/129030"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"21","page_end":"18","year":2015,"page_start":"13","journal_title":"Grav.Cosmol."},"title":{"title":"Are black holes totally black?"},"misc":["and Yu. V. Pavlov"],"authors":[{"full_name":"Grib, A.A."}]},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1323062"}}],"number_of_pages":4,"referenced_authors_bais":["Y.Wu.28","S.D.Maharaj.1","R.Goswami.2","S.M.Wagh.1","B.C.Nolan.1","F.J.Tipler.1","An.Zhong.Wang.1","A.A.Grib.1","Y.V.Pavlov.1","M.D.Mkenyeleye.1","V.Vertogradov.1"],"legacy_version":"20180607214511.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Math and Math Physics"}],"legacy_creation_date":"2016-05-27","author_count":1,"first_author":{"full_name":"Vertogradov, V.D.","last_name":"Vertogradov","first_name":"V.D.","recid":2373188},"control_number":1465928,"dois":[{"source":"Springer","value":"10.1134/S020228931602016X"}],"earliest_date":"2016-05-25","document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Vertogradov:2016gqc"],"abstracts":[{"source":"Springer","value":"The gravitational collapse in generalized Vaidya space-time is considered. It is known that the end state of gravitational collapse, as to whether a black hole or a naked singularity is formed, depends on the mass function M(v, r). Here we give conditions for the mass function which correspond to the equation of the state P = αρ. where α ∈ (0, 1/3], and according to these conditions we obtain either a black hole or a naked singularity as the end state of gravitational collapse. We also give the conditions for the mass function under which the singularity is gravitationally strong. We present simple examples showing when the result of gravitational collapse is a naked singularity and when this singularity is strong.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"Springer"}}],"refereed":true,"titles":[{"title":"Naked singularity formation in generalized Vaidya space-time"}],"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"MSNET","value":"3505077"}],"facet_author_name":["2373188_V.D. Vertogradov"],"imprints":[{"date":"2016-05-25"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1465928","updated":"2023-03-06T23:33:30.889626"},"curated":false,"journal_title_variants":["Grav.Cosmol.","Grav. Cosmol."]},"created":"2016-05-27T00:00:00+00:00","id":"1465928","updated":"2023-03-06T23:33:30.889626+00:00","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1465928?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1465928"}},{"metadata":{"citation_count":5,"citation_count_without_self_citations":4,"authors":[{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/03ht1xw27"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"barve, sukratu","full_name":"Barve, Sukratu","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1017201"},"last_name":"Barve","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903253"},"value":"Tata Inst."}],"ids":[{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"S.Barve.1"}],"signature_block":"BARVs","first_name":"Sukratu","uuid":"8d86e901-b21d-4929-b921-aad26bbf12c6","recid":1017201},{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/03ht1xw27"}],"full_name_unicode_normalized":"singh, t.p.","full_name":"Singh, T.P.","curated_relation":true,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/988555"},"last_name":"Singh","affiliations":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903253"},"value":"Tata Inst."}],"ids":[{"schema":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1862-1505"},{"schema":"INSPIRE BAI","value":"T.P.Singh.1"}],"signature_block":"SANGHt","first_name":"T.P.","uuid":"e4b8a902-50a1-4846-aac1-1198392a48fc","recid":988555}],"citeable":true,"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","references":[{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"2","artid":"99","page_start":"99","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/225746"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"4","artid":"1457","page_start":"1457","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."}}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"443","artid":"473","page_start":"473","journal_title":"Proc.Roy.Soc.Lond.A"}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/349479"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"443","artid":"493","page_start":"493","journal_title":"Proc.Roy.Soc.Lond.A"}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/349489"}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"gr-qc/9303037","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"47","artid":"5357","page_start":"5357","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D"}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/34598"}},{"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"gr-qc/9409062","publication_info":{"journal_volume":"13","artid":"559","page_start":"559","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/377529"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"26","artid":"751","page_start":"751","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D"}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/182785"}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"journal_volume":"6","artid":"1599","page_start":"1599","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav."}},"curated_relation":false,"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/292562"}}],"number_of_pages":11,"referenced_authors_bais":["J.Wainwright.1","G.Williams.2","P.S.Joshi.1","S.W.Goode.1","T.P.Singh.1","D.M.Eardley.1","R.P.A.C.Newman.1","I.H.Dwivedi.1","W.A.Hiscock.1"],"legacy_version":"20170111105946.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"}],"legacy_creation_date":"1997-05-23","preprint_date":"1997-05","author_count":2,"first_author":{"affiliations_identifiers":[{"schema":"ROR","value":"https://ror.org/03ht1xw27"}],"full_name":"Barve, Sukratu","last_name":"Barve","first_name":"Sukratu","recid":1017201},"control_number":443358,"earliest_date":"1997-05","document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Barve:1997nb"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"We examine the growth of the Weyl curvature in two examples of naked singularity formation in spherical gravitational collapse - dust and the Vaidya spacetime. We find that the Weyl scalar diverges along outgoing radial null geodesics as they meet the naked singularity in the past. The implications of this result for the Weyl curvature hypothesis are discussed. We mention the possibility that although classical general relativity admits naked singularity solutions arising from gravitational collapse, the second law of thermodynamics could forbid their occurrence in nature. The method can also be used to compare the relative importance of initial data and that of the energy-momentum tensor in deciding the metric solution in any general case.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"title":"Naked singularities and the Weyl curvature hypothesis"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Naked singularities and the Weyl Curvature Hypothesis"}],"external_system_identifiers":[{"schema":"SPIRES","value":"SPIRES-3556824"}],"facet_author_name":["988555_Tejinder Pal Singh","1017201_Sukratu Barve"],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:443358","updated":"2023-03-06T15:12:18.835084"},"curated":true,"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["gr-qc"],"value":"gr-qc/9705060"}]},"created":"1997-05-23T00:00:00+00:00","id":"443358","updated":"2023-03-06T15:12:18.835084+00:00","links":{